# AfProsPos — production root .htaccess
#
# Use this ONLY if your host deploys the whole Laravel project (this file's
# folder) as the web root and you cannot point the domain's document root
# directly at the `public/` folder (typical on shared/cPanel hosting).
#
# If you control the document root (VPS, Docker, managed Laravel hosting),
# do NOT use this file — point the web server's document root straight at
# `public/` instead and skip this entirely. That's the more secure setup;
# this file is a compatibility shim for hosts that don't allow it.
#
# What it does: forwards every request into public/, where Laravel's own
# public/.htaccess takes over (front controller routing, static assets,
# etc. — already present in this repo, untouched). None of .env, vendor/,
# domain/, config/, routes/, storage/logs, etc. exist as real files under
# public/, so the forwarding rule alone already 404s direct requests for
# them — this section only adds an explicit, faster-failing block for the
# small set of dotfiles/composer/package manifests as defense-in-depth.
#
# Deliberately NOT blocking /storage/* here: repair photos, product
# images, and anything else uploaded to the "public" disk are served at
# exactly that URL via the storage:link symlink (public/storage ->
# storage/app/public) — blocking it outright would 403 real app features.

# ---------- Block direct access to a few sensitive root-level files ----------
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
    RewriteEngine On

    RewriteRule ^(\.env.*|\.git.*|composer\.(json|lock)|package(-lock)?\.json|\.htaccess)$ - [F,L]
</IfModule>

<IfModule !mod_rewrite.c>
    # Fallback for the same paths if mod_rewrite is unavailable
    <FilesMatch "^(\.env.*|\.git.*|composer\.(json|lock)|package(-lock)?\.json)$">
        Require all denied
    </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

Options -Indexes -MultiViews

# ---------- Optional: force HTTPS ----------
# Uncomment once TLS is confirmed working end-to-end (some hosts terminate
# SSL before Apache ever sees the request, which makes %{HTTPS} unreliable —
# test this on your actual host before relying on it).
# <IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
#     RewriteCond %{HTTPS} off
#     RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://%{HTTP_HOST}%{REQUEST_URI} [L,R=301]
# </IfModule>

# ---------- Forward everything else into public/ ----------
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} !^/public/
    RewriteRule ^(.*)$ public/$1 [L]
</IfModule>
